post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'

==> tpm-trust: TPM Authenticity Verifier
    Validates your TPM's Endorsement Key (EK) certificate against a trusted
    bundle of manufacturer root CAs. All operations are read-only — nothing
    is written to the TPM. Requires TPM 2.0 and an internet connection on
    first run (to download the trust bundle and CRLs).

  QUICK START
  -----------
  1. Verify your TPM is genuine:

       tpm-trust audit

     On Linux, sudo is requested automatically if TPM access requires it.
     Exit code 0 = trusted, 1 = untrusted or error.

  2. Verbose output (shows each validation step):

       tpm-trust audit --verbose

  3. Skip CRL revocation checking (offline or if CRL endpoints are down):

       tpm-trust audit --skip-revocation-check

  4. Audit a specific key type:

       tpm-trust audit rsa-2048
       tpm-trust audit ecc-nist-p256

     Available key types:
       rsa-2048, rsa-3072, rsa-4096
       ecc-nist-p256, ecc-nist-p384, ecc-nist-p521, ecc-sm2-p256

  INSPECT CERTIFICATES
  --------------------
  5. List all EK certificates available in the TPM:

       tpm-trust certificates list

  6. Display a specific EK certificate:

       tpm-trust certificates get rsa-2048
       tpm-trust certificates get rsa-2048 --format pem
       tpm-trust certificates get rsa-2048 --short
       tpm-trust certificates get rsa-2048 --bundle    # full chain

  TPM INFORMATION
  ---------------
  7. Display TPM hardware details (vendor, firmware, algorithms, PCR banks):

       tpm-trust info
       tpm-trust info --format json

  TROUBLESHOOTING
  ---------------
  - "no EK certificates available in TPM":
    Your TPM's NV storage has no pre-provisioned EK cert. AMD and Intel
    fTPMs fall back to fetching the cert from the manufacturer's URL
    automatically. Other vendors may not support this.

  - "unsupported manufacturer":
    The manufacturer's root CA is not yet in tpm-ca-certificates. Open an
    issue at: https://github.com/loicsikidi/tpm-ca-certificates/issues/new

  - "TPM is not genuine":
    The EK certificate did not chain to any trusted manufacturer root.
    This could indicate a counterfeit TPM, a provisioning error, or a
    manufacturer not yet included in the trust bundle.

  - Permission errors:
    tpm-trust needs access to /dev/tpmrm0. It will request sudo
    automatically, or you can add your user to the tss group:
      sudo usermod -aG tss $USER
    Then log out and back in.

  - The trust bundle auto-updates on each run. You do not need to
    update tpm-trust to get the latest manufacturer root CAs.

  See: https://github.com/loicsikidi/tpm-trust

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}
